permalink: /_redirects
sitemap: false
---
{%- comment -%}
  The 301 redirects of the old WordPress URLs, in the format of Cloudflare Pages and
  Netlify (`/from /to 301`, one per line, first match wins), written to /_redirects.
  Jekyll skips source files that start with an underscore, hence this file and its
  permalink. It has no extension on purpose: Jekyll adds the extension of the source
  file to a permalink that does not end in it (redirects.txt would be written as
  _redirects.txt). It is a single file at the site root: it is listed in
  `exclude_from_localization`, so jekyll-polyglot does not build /it/_redirects as well
  (`include: [_redirects]` would not work: an included file ignores the exclusions).
  The data is _data/redirects.yml; each `from` is written twice, with its trailing slash
  and without, the same rule as BuildRedirects.rules in script/build-redirects.rb, which
  also writes the nginx form. script/checks/redirects_emit.rb proves that they agree.
{%- endcomment -%}
# Generated from _data/redirects.yml by redirects. Do not edit.
{% for r in site.data.redirects -%}
{%- assign cut = r.from.size | minus: 1 -%}
{{ r.from }} {{ r.to }} 301
{{ r.from | slice: 0, cut }} {{ r.to }} 301
{% endfor -%}
